An example game that loads an entire world from one prefab and lets the player fly around it.

    temple_explorer [PREFAB] [SKY]

The prefab is "temple.glb", from content/temple.gcpak, unless another prefab's name is given. Any prefab in any of the .gcpak files
in the content directory can be loaded. To make one from a glTF file:

    gcpak_editor --pack temple.glb --out content/temple.gcpak

A glTF scene has no sky, so one is added: it is seen behind the world and lights it (see gc::SkyboxComponent). It is
"mpumalanga_veld_puresky_8k.hdr" unless another sky's name is given, such as "table_mountain_2_puresky_8k.hdr". Skies are made from
the .hdr files in content/textures/skyboxes when content/textures is packaged. The sky is turned so that its sun is in the same
compass direction as the scene's directional light, and is as bright as suits that light.

Controls:
    Mouse               look around
    W A S D             fly forwards, left, backwards, right (forwards is where the camera is looking)
    Space / E           up
    Left Ctrl / Q       down
    Left Shift          fly faster
    Up / Down arrows    double / halve the flying speed
    L                   turn the light on the camera on and off
    I                   turn the sky's lighting on and off. When off, the world is lit by a flat ambient light of the same
                        strength instead, as it was before the engine had skies. The sky is still seen
    C                   turn collisions on and off. When on, the camera can't fly through anything that has a ColliderComponent
                        (everything, in a prefab that gcpak_editor made from a glTF file)
    F10                 show the debug windows and free the mouse
    Escape              quit

The camera starts in front of the world, on its -Y side, looking in, with a flying speed that suits the size of the world.
